 >>> dataset = load_dataset("csv", data_files=data_files)
 ```
 
+## Excel files
+
+🤗 Datasets supports loading Excel workbooks (`.xlsx`, `.xlsm` and the legacy `.xls` format) with the `excel` builder:
+
+```py
+>>> from datasets import load_dataset
+>>> dataset = load_dataset("excel", data_files="my_dataset.xlsx", split="train")
+```
+
+The first worksheet is read by default and its first row is used as the header. Use `sheet_name` to pick another worksheet by name or 0-based index, several worksheets as a list, or all of them with `None` (the rows of the selected sheets are concatenated in order):
+
+```py
+>>> dataset = load_dataset("excel", data_files="my_dataset.xlsx", sheet_name="February", split="train")
+>>> dataset = load_dataset("excel", data_files="my_dataset.xlsx", sheet_name=None, split="train")
+```
+
+All the parsing options of [`pandas.read_excel`](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.read_excel.html) are forwarded, so you can skip preamble rows, rename columns or tune missing value handling:
+
+```py
+>>> dataset = load_dataset("excel", data_files="report.xlsx", skiprows=2, nrows=100, na_values=["unknown"], split="train")
+```
+
+You can also load a workbook directly with [`~datasets.Dataset.from_excel`] and [`~datasets.DatasetDict.from_excel`], and export a dataset to a workbook with [`~datasets.Dataset.to_excel`]:
+
+```py
+>>> from datasets import Dataset
+>>> dataset = Dataset.from_excel("my_dataset.xlsx")
+>>> dataset.to_excel("export.xlsx")
+```
+
+[`~datasets.Dataset.to_excel`] writes a single worksheet (named `"Sheet1"` by default) with a header row, while [`~datasets.DatasetDict.to_excel`] writes every split as one worksheet named after the split:
+
+```py
+>>> dataset_dict.to_excel("all_splits.xlsx")
+```
+
